        "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd">

<html>
  <head>
    <title>R&oslash;vhult - An internationally know cardgame (aka Arsehole, ...)</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Documentation of the R&oslash;vhult cardgame">
    <meta name="keywords" content="Documentation, Docu, Rovhult, R&oslash;vhult; game, card, cardgame">

    <meta name="DC.Creator" content="Markus Schwab">
    <meta name="DC.Date" content="2002-07-20">
    <meta name="DC.Rights" content="Copyright (C) 2002 - 2006, distributed under the GNU Free Documentation License">
  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">R&oslash;vhult</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>R&oslash;vhult is a cardgame I learned from three danes I was travelling
      with; hence the danish name and this lovely, unpronounceable letter
      (sounds a bit like the first letters in &laquo;earth&raquo;, while you
      are vomitting.) Other names for the games are Shithead (UK) or Asshole
      (Australia, USA).</p>

    <p>The goal of the game is to get rid of all your cards, according to the
      following - quite simple as also Yankees are playing it ;) - rules:</p>

    <p>Every player starts with three cards in his hand, and two times three
      cards on the table (where the lower three are face down (hidden) and
      the upper ones are visible).</p>

    <p>The visible cards on the table can be exchanged with the cards in the
      hand (with drag and drop <a
      href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>). Generally people try to
      put &quot;good&quot; cards (according to the rules below) on the table,
      but your milage might vary.</p>

    <p>To actually start the game, click on the card you want to play. The cards
      are sorted and the clicked card is played.</p>

    <p>In counter-clockwise turns the players put down their cards, which
      must be equal or bigger than the last played one. Note that you
      can put down any amount of cards with equal numbers (e.g. the
      &spades; 5 and the &clubs; 5). After playing the player must pick
      up cards from the staple until he has again three cards in the
      hand (or the staple is empty).</p>

    <p>There are a few special cards and exceptions:</p>
    <dl>
      <dt><b>2's</b></dt>
      <dd><p>Can be played always; the next player can continue with every
          card</p></dd>
      <dt><b>7 is the last card played</b></dt>
      <dd><p>The next card must be equal or smaller</p></dd>
      <dt><b>8's</b></dt>
      <dd><p>The next player is skipped</p></dd>
      <dt><b>10's</b></dt>
      <dd><p>Can be played always (as 2's), but additionally the staple of
          played cards is removed and the player can continue (though he may
          not pick up cards from the staple (except he has no more cards to
          play).</p></dd>
      <dt><b>Four cards with the same number on the staple</b></dt>
      <dd><p>If the last four played cards are equal the whole played staple is
          removed and the same player can continue (having an effect similar to
          playing a 10, except that you can pick up cards). Note that also the
          effect of "special cards" are removed (e.g. the next player is not
          skipped, if there have been played four 8's).</p></dd>
    </dl>

    <p>If a player can't continue according to those rules he must pick up
      all of the played cards. Of course the played pile can be picked up
      always willingly; making the next player continue.</p>

    <p>If a player has no card left in his hand (and there are no cards left
      on the staple), he can continue with the cards on the table. They
      are played according to the same rules as from the hand; starting
      first with the visible ones and then - if they are also sucessfully
      removed - with the invisible ones.</p>

    <p>Note that if you have to pick up the played pile while playing from
      hand you can't move none of your visible cards from the table into
      those in your hand and that you have to play first the (new) cards
      in your hand before you can (again) play the card from the table.</p>

    <p>The invisible cards have to be played randomly. If the selected card
      can't be played according to the rules, it and all the cards from the
      pile must be picked up.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The staple: A heap of cards (with face down), from where you put
        up new cards (if you put the mouse over it you are informed about
        the remaining cards).</li>
     <li>The cards of the human player; above the cards in the hand, below
        the cards on the table.</li>
      <li>In the next line it's the same with computer players 1 and 3. In
        between them there's the played pile (which also shows the number of
        the cards inside, is the mouse rests over it).</li>
      <li>In the last line there's computer player 2</li>
    </ul>

    <hr size=1 noshade>
    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note1"></a>1)
      Drag and drop means clicking with the a mouse button on the card,
      holding the button and then dragging the card to its destination.
      The end of this activity is signaled by releasing the button.</p>

